From 68054f24d0996dacee2780055575e4cfb46a272f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian MacDonald <ian@netstatz.com>
Date: Tue, 29 Sep 2026 18:02:13 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 37/88] feat(server): download large model files over parallel
 Range requests

A model file is fetched as one sequential HTTP stream. Measured on
Hugging Face's CDN that leaves most of the link idle: one stream moved
29-59 MiB/s while 8 concurrent ranged GETs of the same file moved
196-455 MiB/s on the same boxes.

Model downloads of 64 MiB or more now split the remaining bytes into
chunks and fetch them over download_connections (config.json, default 8,
1-32, applied at runtime via /internal/set) concurrent Range requests,
each written straight to its offset in the .partial file. A one-byte
probe (Range: 0-0) decides eligibility; a server that answers without
206 + Content-Range, a file under the threshold, download_connections=1,
or an active download_rate_limit (enforced per transfer and by
serialising downloads, so N connections would multiply it) keeps today's
single stream. Backend installs do not opt in.

curl_multi rather than N threads: one thread drives every range, so the
progress callback, cancellation (callback or g_download_cancelled), the
per-transfer stall timeout and all file writes stay on the caller's
thread with no locks, exactly where the single-stream path runs them.
Multiplexing is disabled so each range gets its own connection instead
of sharing one HTTP/2 flow-control window.

Resume safety: out-of-order writes break the "partial size = bytes
written" rule every resume relies on, so while a parallel attempt runs,
<partial>.ranges records the contiguous prefix known to be written. It
is created before the first out-of-order byte and advanced as leading
chunks complete. A failed, stalled or cancelled attempt, and any later
download_file call that finds the journal after a crash, truncates the
partial back to that prefix and removes the journal, so both the
parallel and single-stream paths resume from honest bytes. Each range
must return 206 with the exact Content-Range and byte count or the whole
attempt fails; the existing hash check remains the completeness gate.
Memory stays bounded to curl and stdio buffers.

Chunks are sized remaining / (4 * connections), clamped to
[threshold / 4, 256 MiB], which bounds the work a crash can discard.

